{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And HaShem has declared you, etc.</b> Its saying <b>to be to Him a treasured people</b> means that even if another nation were to come and perfect its deeds and strive to cling to the Shekhinah — they would not attain the level of Israel, and this quality is called סגלה (treasured property), and this is their greatness. And its saying <b>as He spoke to you</b> refers to the event of Mount Sinai, where it is written (Exodus 19:5): ״וִהְיִיתֶם לִי סְגֻלָּה מִכָּל הָעַמִּים״." "<b>Or</b> it may intend that even though there may come a time when Israel provokes the Creator, even so He will not exchange them for another nation. And its saying <b>as He spoke</b> is as they said, may their memory be for blessing (Berakhot 7a), on the verse (Exodus 33:16): ״וְנִפְלִינוּ אֲנִי וְעַמְּךָ״ — that Moses asked HaShem not to cause His Shekhinah to rest upon the nations, and we explained it in its place (Hefetz HaShem) that the intention is even if the deeds of a nation from among the nations are refined and Israel are transgressors of His will, HaShem agreed and spoke to act in accordance with Moses' words." "<b>And to keep all His commandments.</b> One must raise a difficulty: is this not a matter of the conditions of honor that Israel recorded toward HaShem, as written in the verse ״אֶת ה׳ הֶאֱמַרְתָּ״? What place is there to mention it among the matters from HaShem to Israel?" "<b>Or perhaps</b> the glory of Israel is that which elevated and exalted them by giving them all the commandments — something He did not do for the earlier ones: for although He gave His commandments, blessed be He, to Adam, to Noah, and to the Patriarchs, He gave only two or three grains; but to Israel He gave the keeping of all His commandments, blessed be He. And you should know that all the commandments are nothing but a guarding from contamination and from the soul's defilement, and through the perfection of keeping the commandments a person will be complete of every blemish and of every defilement." "<b>2)</b> It further intends that HaShem strengthens their arms to keep all the commandments, for not everyone merits this thing — even if they wish to keep them, the opportunity may not present itself to them; and if it presents itself, their capacity may not suffice; and if their capacity suffices for part of the commandments, it may not suffice for all the commandments. And it says here that HaShem declared them to keep all His commandments." "<b>(3)</b> It further intends, in the manner of the verse's saying (Ecclesiastes 8:5): ״שׁוֹמֵר מִצְוָה לֹא יֵדַע דָּבָר רָע״, and they said, of blessed memory (Pesachim 8a), that those dispatched for a mitzvah suffer no harm, and this is the meaning of the verse's statement here <b>and to keep</b> — meaning, HaShem will keep all his mitzvot so that no harmful thing may issue from them." "<b>(4)</b> It further intends to point out to them a thing that even Moses himself, peace be upon him, did not attain, notwithstanding his being included within the category of a treasured people. And it is in the manner of their statement, of blessed memory (Sotah 14a), regarding the prayer of Moses to enter the land: Was it merely to eat of its fruits, etc.? Rather Moses said: There are many mitzvot that depend on the Land, etc. — end quote. And this is what he intended to say to them: See your excellence, that HaShem has set you apart, etc., <b>and to keep all His mitzvot</b>; and therefore he was precise in saying <b>He has set you apart today</b> — meaning, at this time when you are entering the Land, and there you will keep all the mitzvot. And this is an excellence that even Moses did not attain." "<b>Now, you will find</b> six excellences in the verse <b>et HaShem you have affirmed</b>: 1 — to be for you as Elohim; 2 — to walk in His ways; 3 — to keep His statutes; 4 — and His commandments; 5 — and His ordinances; 6 — and to listen to His voice. Correspondingly, HaShem stated six excellences for the congregation of Israel: 1 — to be for Him a treasured people — this corresponds to 'to be for you as Elohim'; 2 — and to keep all His mitzvot — this corresponds to 'to walk in His ways'; 3, 4, and 5 — and to set you supreme, etc., for praise and for name and for glory — these correspond to the keeping of His statutes and His commandments and His ordinances; 6 — and to be for you a holy people to HaShem — this corresponds to 'and to listen to His voice,' for through engagement with the Torah this name of 'holy' will be ascribed to them. And His saying <b>as He spoke</b> — as it is written (Exodus 19:6): ״וְאַתֶּם תִּהְיוּ לִי וְגוֹ׳ וְגוֹי קָדוֹשׁ, אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר תְּדַבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {7 "The lemma head in [0] and [1] carries a paragraph marker (ג׳/ד׳) with a parenthetical; I rendered the reversed parenthetical as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:35.191295829Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}